From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fernando Fiori <ffiori@microsoft.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 16:12:54 -0700
Subject: [highlights] Keep hit-test range pointers stable

highlightsFromPoint() retains a reference to a Highlight range set entry
while computing its bounds. Geometry computation can run script that
adds ranges, reallocating the set's backing storage and leaving the
reference dangling.

Copy the AbstractRange pointer before computing geometry, rather than
referencing the Member in the set. The set's iterators remain valid when
adding ranges, so this preserves live iteration while keeping the
current range safe to use.

Bug: 576984708
Change-Id: Idfe994d6b6eb7985aab356e47df32f0b19ce61e2
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8640466
Reviewed-by: Stefan Zager <szager@chromium.org>
Commit-Queue: Fernando Fiori <ffiori@microsoft.com>
Cr-Commit-Position: refs/heads/main@{#1726331}

Electron adaptation notes (43-x-y):
- M150's HighlightsFromPoint() loop body predates the ResolveEphemeralRange()
  / NodeRange refactor on main. Only the actual fix is carried: the loop
  variable is changed from a reference into highlight->GetRanges() to a copied
  AbstractRange* so the pointer stays valid if the HeapVector is mutated, and
  the .Get() call on it is dropped accordingly. The branch's loop body is
  otherwise unchanged.

